From: Christina M Grimes <cg39+@andrew.cmu.edu>
Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc.

Sorry, I didn't mean to hit "send"!! I was only halfway through that
message, and never even wrote a suitable opener... oh well... Here goes
again:

Ok, maybe one more post... :)

Excerpts from mail: 9-Sep-93 PHIL: Ethics etc. by "James A. Donald"@infose 
> The social contract theory is probably valid for systems
> of justice and judgment, and details of property rights
> and the like, but rights cannot be derived solely from
> contract, or solely from custom, because contract assumes a
> pre-existing principle that contracts should be honored,
> that they should be for value, and so on and so forth.

I'm not sure what this line of reasoning is or implies; perhaps I should
ask for a clarification. The argument seems to be that if rights came
simply from contract, there would be no reason necessarily to honor that
contract, and then anything goes: rape, torture, etc. Therefore, we need
a solid, objective set of rights.

I share the desire for objectivity, but am still not sure if rights
themselves are objective, even if _which_ rights intelligent beings
deserve can be determined objectively. I'll use a scenario to illustrate
why I think this:

A person is alone in the middle of the Pacific on a rather large island
with plenty of food, fresh water, etc. This person, having lived alone
for upwards of 10 years, has no need for rights, since the animals that
live on the island are either companions, food, or ignored. He may or
may not *have* rights, but the question is irrelevant. (With apologies
to F.E.S., I'm going to assume that only humans hold rights.)

That year, a shipwreck victim is washed ashore. He meets the original
islander later that day. The two of them have a mutual choice: 1) to
coexist, cooperate, and otherwise get along, in order to have a better
chance of surviving and getting off the island. 2) They have a severe
distaste for each other, and decide to live on separate sides of the
island and not bother each other. 3) They may beome bitter enemies
(though I can't imagine why), and be at each others' throats,
constructing elaborate defenses and traps on their own sides of the
island. 

In the first case, the two recognize each other's rights in the process
of deciding to get along and work to mutual benefit. In the second case,
they recognize each others' rights, but only as a way to avoid
destructive conflict. In both of those two cases, the recognizing of
rights is convenient, and even if the rights *are* objective, the
islanders don't care. They do what works. In the third case, the two
don't recognize each others' rights. Barring long-ingrained scruples
against wonton killing, the only hesitation they might have for getting
rid of the other is protection of their own lives, which would be
endangered in the conflict. Whether or not the rights still exist is
open to debate, but they might as well not exist, if they're not
recognized.

To me, this all points to a theory of rights as a contract that works to
mutual benefit. In a rational society, we assume no one would act as the
islanders in scenario 3), because it is simply destructive and
irrational; therefore, all dealings with others would be theoretically
based on rights. This makes rights universal, but not necesarily
objective.

You can draw a kind of parallel with concepts. Concepts are a human
invention, that is, the invention of intelligent, rational creatures.
They are not "objective" only in the sense that we did not discover
them, but invented them: similarly, we did not discover rights, but we
invented them. This does not lower the validity of either. Both rest on
objective foundations, but are stilll our own convention. We could also
do away with concepts if we wanted to, just as we could do away with
rights. This does not mean that that would be in any way constructive or
useful. It is just to emphasize that both are our invention, and
dependent on whether or not it serves our purpose by being useful and
constructive.

> If you argue rights from contract, then the nihilists at
> Loompanics will correctly argue that there is no contract
> to honor contract, therefore anything is permitted,
> including rape and torture, and the democratic... [long chain of reasoning
> that arrives at disastrous results]

While it is true that once a rights contract has been accepted, there is
no guarantee that either will or must follow it, it does not follow that
anything is permissible. What gives the contract of rights validity is
that those in the contract *defend* their rights against attacks. Such
as when a person accidentally spills Coke all over another's keyboard,
the keyboard owner asks for restitution. If the coke-spiller is believes
in rights, se will promptly cough up the dough. However, if se says,
"Yeah right, dickhead, who's gonna make me?" the keyboard owner will
show sem just who's going to make sem, possibly by threatening to extort
the money until the offender agrees to seek arbitration or relents and
hands over the cash or, if this fails, actually taking the money. The
elegance of the contract is that by denying the validity of rights, you
also must deny the validity of your own. This is (theoretically) the
purpose of the goverment today: to defend those who can't stand up for
their own rights. However, I picture an Extropian society where killing
is mere vandalism, everyone has adequate power to defend themselves, but
it's not necessary because people don't bother to violate rights, since
it's not constructive and there's no point to it.

> To argue that the social contract rests on the social
> contract, as Perry does, is to build on sand.  The nature
> of man provides a more solid foundation.

I don't remember Perry ever saying the social contract rests on social
contract. The idea, I think, was that you don't respect rights because
you "Should", but because it's rational and because you back up your own
with more than words. I agree with you that the nature of man does
provide a solid foundation. This is precisely what the contract is built
on.

> We have the right to defend ourselves and our
> property, because of the kind of animals that we
> are.  True law derives from this right, not from
> the arbitrary power of the omnipotent state.

Why do we have the right to defend ourselves? Because we can and will;
no one can tell us otherwise. I agree with you about the origin of state
power, and for now, concede that law is necessary to protect rights. But
I still look forward to when anarchy is not only possible, but the best
possible system.

--Ratha 

