Oh, this already exists.  Jay Prime Positive created one for Doug Platt, so
folks can learn what chord keying is like without having to buy a hardware
chord keyboard.  Jay's driver replaces the DOS keyboard device driver and
lets you map a chord keyboard onto a subset of the keys of a normal
keyboard (8-9-0-[ for the fingers and V-B-N for the thumb, for example) and
type chords on the standard keyboard.  You can also set up any set of chord
mnemonics you want (there are several different standards for mapping
chords to characters).

I don't know if the software is commercially available yet or not.
