Date: Thu, 9 Sep 93 20:42:44 PDT
From: fschulz@pyramid.com
Subject: Tech: chord keyboard

Peter Baumbach writes
---------
Instead of a keyboard, make a glove.  On the fingertips put piezoelectric
plastic sensors.  You can then "type" against any surface.  Alternately,
you can sew the plastic at the joints of the glove.  You can "type" by
bending your fingers.  If I remember right, the piezo film is sold by
the name of Kynar film.  If you like, I can find out for sure.

I came up with this idea while driving.  I want to be able to type and
drive at the same time.
---------


I want to be able to type whenever I am awake. Direct neural input
is what I want.

The piezo film would do me no good. I have no analog design skills.
I would need a packaged device with digital outputs which could
be plugged into a microcontroller. I can build the rest from there.

Also, more than 1 bit per finger would be nice. 5 bits does not
encode very much. My keypad used 4 fingers (no thumb) and encoded
2 bits per finger. Each finger had 3 keys to hit on a 4 by 3 matrix.
This gives 2**8 - 1 = 255 codes per chord. I got a prototype estimate
of $3000 for this keypad. I practiced typing on an imaginary keyboard
(the side of an Altoids box) to see if the design geometry was sound.
I put in 40 hours of typing on the Altoids box (an English mint).
The keypad surface is 2 by 6 centimeters. I found the key force
needs to be under 30 grams.

A dataglove type device would also work. I have practiced
making tiny fast movements that a dataglove could sense.
The dataglove senses finger joint bending, using force sensing resistors.
2 bits per finger is easy to encode. The dataglove cost is $10,000.

Even better would be to sense EMG. Just think about moving.
The cost of usable sensors is probably much worse than for the dataglove.
-- 
	Frank Schulz ( fschulz@pyramid.com )

